public List<List<Integer>> permute(int[] nums) {
    List<List<Integer>> result = new ArrayList<>();
    backtrack(nums, result, new ArrayList<>(), 0);
    return result;
}

private void backtrack(int[] nums, List<List<Integer>> result, List<Integer> current, int begin) {
    if (begin == nums.length) {
        result.add(new ArrayList<>(current));
        return;
    }
    for (int i = begin; i < nums.length; i++) {
        current.add(nums[i]);
        backtrack(nums, result, current, i + 1);
        current.remove(current.size() - 1);
    }
}
```
This implementation uses backtracking to generate all possible permutations of the input array. It uses a recursive approach by calling the backtrack function for each possible value that can be used as the first element of the permutation, and recursively explores all possible combinations of the remaining elements in the array. The backtrack function adds the current permutation to the result list and then recursively calls itself for each possible value that can be used as the first element of the next permutation, and so on. Finally, the result list is returned by the permute function.

